1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,391
Many harbor beans are concrete classes
with no interface. A @Service that prints
gate passes, a @Component that scores tide
windows — injected by their own type.

2
00:00:10,511 --> 00:00:22,223
JDK’s Proxy cannot wrap those: it only
implements interfaces. Spring’s answer is
a class-based proxy via CGLIB (and related
subclass generation in modern Spring).

3
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:29,951
Runtime bytecode creates a subclass of
your concrete class, overrides methods,
and weaves interceptor calls into those
overrides.

4
00:00:30,071 --> 00:00:43,223
The object you inject is-a your class — a
subclass instance — while still delegating
through the advice chain. Use a concrete
gate helper on purpose — no interface in
sight. Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:43,343 --> 00:00:59,759
Look at this java for a moment.
Collaborators inject GatePassPrinter. With
CGLIB, the runtime type might be
GatePassPrinter$$SpringCGLIB$$0 — a
subclass. instanceof GatePassPrinter is
true.

6
00:00:59,879 --> 00:01:15,455
Casting to the concrete type works. render
is overridden so entering it runs the
audit @Before, then the original body.
Same class shape for injection; extra
behavior in overridden methods. Trade-offs
arrive immediately.

7
00:01:15,575 --> 00:01:28,967
Final classes cannot be subclassed — proxy
creation fails. Final methods cannot be
overridden — calls go straight to the
original implementation without advice.
Private methods are not overridden either.

8
00:01:29,087 --> 00:01:40,919
Constructors run for the subclass; side
effects in constructors can surprise you
when the proxy is created. Equals and
hashCode sometimes need care because proxy
and target are distinct objects.

9
00:01:41,039 --> 00:01:54,647
Compared with JDK proxies: interface
wrapper versus subclass wrapper.
GateService programmed to an interface
fits JDK. GatePassPrinter programmed as a
concrete @Service fits CGLIB.

10
00:01:54,767 --> 00:02:00,671
Neither is “more Spring.” They are two
strategies for the same AOP idea.

11
00:02:00,791 --> 00:02:11,447
Forcing proxyTargetClass = true prefers
CGLIB even when interfaces exist — useful
when code casts to concrete types, costly
when you wanted interface-only wrappers.

12
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:26,111
Your debugging questions stay stable
across Spring versions. Is this bean a JDK
proxy or a CGLIB subclass? Which methods
are actually overridden? Did I call
through the proxy or via this?

13
00:02:26,291 --> 00:02:40,931
A misconception is believing CGLIB
“enhances the original class in place.” It
creates a new subclass instance. Another
is assuming every method call on that
instance is advised — final and private
paths punch holes.

14
00:02:41,051 --> 00:02:54,395
A third is ignoring constructor cost when
thousands of class proxies materialize at
startup. We know how the wrapper is built.
Next is what kinds of logic you hang on it
— the shapes of advice themselves.

15
00:02:54,515 --> 00:03:00,107
Before, after, around, on throw, on return
— that vocabulary is next.
